From: Kholin <oodzchen@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: nt36536: keep the supplies on across unprepare

After the panel supplies are switched off and on at runtime (screen
blank, mode change) the panel accepts the on-commands, reports power mode
0x9e with no DSI errors, and still shows no picture. The bootloader sets
up some panel state that survives the reset pin but not a power cycle,
and the on-commands do not recreate it. At boot the panel is never
unpowered, which is why the first prepare works.

Keep vddio and the KTZ8868 bias enabled after the first prepare: unprepare
sends display off and sleep in, prepare resets the panel and sends the
on-commands again. Verified with repeated blank/unblank cycles on
OPD2413. Costs the panel's sleep-mode current while the screen is off.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt36536.c	2026-11-11 01:21:12.009657522 +0800
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt36536.c	2026-11-11 01:21:12.026602343 +0800
@@ -24,6 +24,8 @@
 struct novatek_nt36536 {
 	struct drm_panel panel;
 	struct mipi_dsi_device *dsi[2];
+	/* Supplies stay on from the first prepare; see novatek_nt36536_unprepare(). */
+	bool powered;
 	struct drm_dsc_config dsc;
 	struct gpio_desc *reset_gpio;
 	struct regulator *vddio;
@@ -252,9 +254,12 @@
 	struct mipi_dsi_multi_context dsi_ctx = { .dsi = ctx->dsi[1] };
 	int ret;
 
-	ret = novatek_nt36536_power_on(ctx);
-	if (ret < 0)
-		return ret;
+	if (!ctx->powered) {
+		ret = novatek_nt36536_power_on(ctx);
+		if (ret < 0)
+			return ret;
+		ctx->powered = true;
+	}
 
 	novatek_nt36536_reset(ctx);
 
@@ -278,6 +283,7 @@
 
 err:
 	novatek_nt36536_power_off(ctx);
+	ctx->powered = false;
 
 	return ret;
 }
@@ -292,8 +298,14 @@
 	if (ret < 0)
 		dev_err(dev, "failed to off panel: %d\n", ret);
 
-	novatek_nt36536_power_off(ctx);
-
+	/*
+	 * Keep vddio and the KTZ8868 bias on. After a full power cycle the
+	 * on-commands are accepted (DCS power mode reads back 0x9e, no DSI
+	 * errors) yet the panel stays dark: it loses some state that the
+	 * bootloader set up and a reset alone does not clear. With the
+	 * supplies kept on, display off/sleep in followed by reset and the
+	 * on-commands restores the picture every time, as it does at boot.
+	 */
 	return 0;
 }
 
